# React — naming and performance habits

## Don't prefix non-hooks with `use`

Locals and props named `usePagination` or `useRowSelection` look like Rules-of-Hooks violations.

```tsx
// ❌ Reads like a hook
const usePagination = paginate;

// ✅ Plain boolean
const isPaginated = paginate;
const hasRowSelection = enableRowSelection && getRowId != null;
```

Reserve the `use` prefix for **custom hooks** (`useImportPreviewState`) and React APIs only.

## Async `useEffect` — guard against stale updates

When an effect kicks off an async request, ignore the result if the effect cleaned up or inputs changed:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  let cancelled = false;

  startTransition(async () => {
    const result = await fetchDetails(id);
    if (cancelled) return;
    setDetails(result);
  });

  return () => { cancelled = true; };
}, [id]);
```

## Hot loops — compile once, match many

If the same patterns run per row (regex, rules, parsers), **compile once** outside the row loop:

```ts
// ❌ new RegExp(pattern) for every row × every rule
rows.map((row) => matchCategoryId(row.description, rules));

// ✅ compile rules once, then match
const compiled = compileCategoryRules(rules);
rows.map((row) => matchCategoryIdWithCompiledRules(row.description, compiled));
```

Apply the same idea to any O(rows × rules) setup work — not just categories.
